  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 255 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The nor'easter made \"landfall\" this morning on the \nJersey Shore, continuing to gradually weaken. Due to this and its \ncompleted occlusion, the weakening process will continue. The low \nwill begin to re-enter the synoptic circulation tomorrow and drift \nnortheastward into the Gulf of Maine and the Canadian Maritimes. \nEven though the precipitation shield will become smaller and more \nisolated as it weakens, the showers will continue over the region as \nthe center passes closer. The showers will exit tomorrow night as \nthe low continues to push northeast. Even though the rain will end \nfor Tuesday, the boundary layer flow will transition to \nnorthwesterly and become increasingly blocked as the inversion \nheight lowers, and cloud cover looks to hold thick in many areas, \nespecially across the upslope areas and northern Vermont. Drier air \nwill eventually mix into the lower levels for Wednesday and the sun \nshould finally return for everywhere, though high clouds will begin \nto increase in the afternoon from a storm system moving toward the \nregion from the Midwest. Due to the sunshine, temperatures should \nrise into the 70s for most areas on Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: High pressure builds into our region from the south on \nThursday providing us with the warmest day of the forecast period. \nMaximum temperatures will range through the 70s with some sites \nhaving near record warmth. Thursday night into Friday will bring our \nnext chance for precipitation as a cold front will cross our region \nin that timeframe. Another ridge of surface high pressure will build \ninto our area behind this front bringing additional dry weather, \nthis time with cooler temperatures as the high builds into our area \nfrom Canada.",
